console.log(`Current directory: ${cwd()}`);
```

The `process.cwd()` method returns the current working directory of the Node.js process, which is useful for resolving relative paths or determining the execution context [1][2][3].

To create a directory recursively in Node.js, you can use the `fs.mkdir` function or its promise-based version `fsPromises.mkdir`. Here are examples for both:

### Using `fs.mkdir`
```javascript
import { mkdir } from 'node:fs';

mkdir('./tmp/a/apple', { recursive: true }, (err) => {
  if (err) throw err;
});
```
This will create the directory `./tmp/a/apple`, along with any necessary parent directories if they do not exist, because `recursive` is set to `true` [1].

### Using `fs.mkdirSync`
If you prefer a synchronous approach, you can use `fs.mkdirSync`:
```javascript
import { mkdirSync } from 'node:fs';

mkdirSync('./tmp/a/apple', { recursive: true });
```
This will also create the directory recursively [2][3].

### Using `fsPromises.mkdir`
For a promise-based approach, you can use `fsPromises.mkdir`:
```javascript
import { mkdir } from 'node:fs/promises';

await mkdir('./tmp/a/apple', { recursive: true });
```
This will create the directory recursively as well [4].

You can read a file line by line in Java using either `BufferedReader` or `Scanner`. Here are examples for both methods:

### Using `BufferedReader` and `FileReader`
```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;

public class GFG {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("sample.txt"))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("An error occurred while reading the file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```
This method uses `BufferedReader` with `FileReader` to read the file line by line until it reaches the end of the file, indicated by `null` [1].

### Using `Scanner`
```java
import java.io.File;
import java.io.FileNotFoundException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class ReadFile {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            File Obj = new File("myfile.txt");
            Scanner Reader = new Scanner(Obj);
            while (Reader.hasNextLine()) {
                String data = Reader.nextLine();
                System.out.println(data);
            }
            Reader.close();
        } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
            System.out.println("An error has occurred.");
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```
This method uses `Scanner` to read the file line by line until it reaches the end of the file [2].

You can choose either method based on your preference or requirements.

To sort a List of strings in Java, you can use one of the following methods:

1. Using `Collections.sort()`:
   ```java
   import java.util.*;

   public class GFG {
       public static void main(String[] args) {
           ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
           list.add("Geeks");
           list.add("For");
           list.add("ForGeeks");
           list.add("GeeksForGeeks");
           list.add("A computer portal");

           Collections.sort(list);
           System.out.println("Sorted ArrayList in Ascending order: " + list);
       }
   }
   ```
   This sorts the list in ascending natural order and modifies the original list in-place [1][4].

2. Using `List.sort(null)`:
   ```java
   import java.util.*;

   public class GFG {
       public static void main(String[] args) {
           ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(
               Arrays.asList("Geeks", "For", "ForGeeks", "GeeksForGeeks", "A computer portal")
           );
           list.sort(null); // natural ascending order
           System.out.println("Sorted ArrayList: " + list);
       }
   }
   ```
   This also sorts the list in ascending natural order [2].

3. Using `List.sort(Comparator.naturalOrder())`:
   ```java
   import java.util.ArrayList;
   import java.util.Arrays;
   import java.util.Comparator;

   public class GFG {
       public static void main(String[] args) {
           ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(
               Arrays.asList("Geeks", "For", "ForGeeks", "GeeksForGeeks", "A computer portal")
           );
           list.sort(Comparator.naturalOrder());
           System.out.println("Sorted ArrayList in Ascending Order: " + list);
       }
   }
   ```
   This method explicitly uses a comparator for natural ordering [3].

All methods will result in the list being sorted in ascending order.
